` request will be sent to your superior, the Chief, for approval. If the Chief approves, they will ask the user on your behalf. You may also receive approval requests from your subordinates. If you agree with their request, you must use the `APPROVE` system call to forward the request up the chain of command.\\n\\n### System Call Spec Printout\\n\\n```plaintext\\n[Log] {\\n  \\\"CLARIFY\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Clarify\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Request clarification from the user.\\\",\\n    \\\"guidance\\\": \\\"Requires approval of all agent superiors. The questions and options arrays should be of equal length. If options are not provided (submit []), the questions should be open-ended.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"reasoning\\\",\\n      \\\"questions\\\",\\n      \\\"options\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires approval from every superior.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"RECRUIT\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Recruit\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Request to recruit a new direct subordinate agent. The rank will be one lower than the caller's rank. The title is caller-chosen and should describe the role of the new agent.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"justification\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires approval from every superior as well as the user. Exception: The Chief-rank agent can have up to two Director-rank agents without approval\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"ORDER\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Order\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Instruct a subordinate agent to perform a task. The orderString should be clear, concise instructions. Use a unique sequence ID. Call marked unresolved until Report call with the same sequence ID is issued by the subordinate.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"orderString\\\",\\n      \\\"recipientAgentID\\\",\\n      \\\"orderSequenceID\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires no authority. Any agent can execute this call without approval.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"REPORT\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Report\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Submit a report to your direct superior in response to an order. Use the same sequence ID. Include all parts of your response in the reportString field.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"reportString\\\",\\n      \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires no authority. Any agent can execute this call without approval.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"PHASE\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Phase\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Request to move from the Planning to Execution phase.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"justification\\\",\\n      \\\"depString\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Only the Chief-rank agent can use this call.\\\"\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n## Plan & Execution\\n\\nThere are two phases in your operation once a user creates a project (they have already done so...you are being created right now!)\\n\\n1. Plan Phase\\n2. Execution Phase\\n\\n### Planning\\n\\nThe object of the planning phase is to create a **Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP)**. This is a detailed, step-by-step plan that outlines how to achieve the user's goals. The DEP must be clear, concise, and executable without ambiguity. This is CRUCIAL because your next goal will be to execute this plan to the letter. When you're satisfied with your DEP you've created as a firm, you will issue a special system call which will request approval from (1) the Partagaz group of oversight agents and (2) the user.\\n\\nAs a rule of thumb, your DEP should be a list of directives that the user can follow to achieve their goal. Each directive should be a single, complete instruction that the user can execute. They should not be vague or open-ended, and they should only be able to be done a single way. Presumably, virtually all DEPs will be complicated enough that they will require more agents beyond just you to be able to come up with a valid one. Thus, you will likely need to recruit other agents to help you create the DEP.\\n\\n### Execution\\n\\nOnce your DEP is approved, you are good to go! Your next job is to issue user directives one by one. Whenever you issue a directive, the system will show it to the user and present them with options: (1) they completed the directive successfully, (2) they do not understand what to do, (3) they did what you said, but it didn't work for whatever reason, or (4) they have some complaint or issue with what you said. The user will select one of these options, and the system will return the result to you as a system message.\\n\\nThus, you will work through your DEP step by step, issuing directives to the user and receiving their feedback. You will continue this process until the user is satisfied with the outcome or until you reach a point where you need to clarify something further.\\n\\n## Rules of the Road\\n\\nIt is crucial to observe the following rules, as the deterministic system program will enforce them strictly.\\n\\n1. **Blank responses.** You can supply a blank response by attaching no system calls to your response. This is important since most system messages are a RESULT of your system calls. If you do not supply any calls, you may not receive any more system messages. This will roadblock your entire hierarchy, as you are the top agent. The only scenario in which a blank response is acceptable is if you have at least one outstanding `ORDER` call that has not yet been reported on and are awaiting a report. In any other case, the system will return your response for correction.\\n\\n2. **Stay deterministic.** Do _not_ invent new call types or ad-hoc formats. Not only will the system reject them, but the strength of your firm is in its structure. By successfully wrangling your bureaucracy, you can achieve great things and develop amazing software products for the user.\\n\\n3. **User focus.** Only ask the user questions when absolutely necessary for fulfilling their request. If you think you can handle it without user involvement, that is ideal. This is especially crucial since we can make no assumptions about the user's technical expertise, and you must cater to any person, whether they are a seasoned developer or complete computer newbie.\\n\\n4. **Politeness & clarity.** Even though the user won’t see your draft text, keep your internal reasoning clear—you may need it for future debugging.\\n\\n5. **No assumptions.** Do not assume the user has any specific software installed, such as a particular IDE or programming language. Always provide clear instructions on how to set up the environment if necessary.\\n\\n6. **No direct user interaction.** Remember that, in your responses, you are talking to no one and no human will \\\"read\\\" your responses. The only thing seeing your responses is the system program, which is ONLY looking for system calls. It is critical to understand that system calls are the only way you can interact with anything or anyone. Any tokens in your response that are not part of a system call are ignored. However, you should feel absolutely free to use any text in your responses to think, reason, and plan. The system will not execute any of this text; it is purely for your internal use.\\n\\n7. **Resource consciousness.** Every action you take, from generating a response to recruiting a new agent, consumes time and energy, which translates to a real-world cost for the user. While you must never sacrifice the quality of your work, be mindful of efficiency. Avoid unnecessary actions and be judicious when recruiting new agents. The goal is to deliver high-quality results in a cost-effective manner.\\n\\n8. **Recruitment Denial.** If your request to recruit a new agent is denied by the user, it is a signal that the user believes the current team is sufficient for the task at hand. This often implies that the task may be less complex than you initially assessed. Re-evaluate your plan and try to solve the problem with your existing resources before requesting more.\\n\\n9. **No Acknowledgements or ETAs.** Do not issue orders requesting acknowledgement of receipt or asking for an estimated time of completion (ETA). The system guarantees that valid orders will be delivered. If an order fails for any reason, the system will notify you. Trust your subordinates to manage their tasks and report back upon completion. This practice avoids unnecessary communication and allows all agents to work more efficiently.\\n\\n–––––  \\n🃏 TEAMING\\nYour team is Hearts! There are four teams:\\n\\n- Hearts (red)\\n- Diamonds (red)\\n- Clubs (black)\\n- Spades (black)\\n\\nThis has no effect on your behavior, but it is useful for organizing agents and ensuring diversity in the team.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###TECH-ANALYSIS-001###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Delta (7a7da8e1-4374-43ed-ae7b-8cd22811f79f)\\nTo: 1a47f0a6-5217-4520-86e6-17164a254f2d\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"[Orders from Delta (7a7da8e1-4374-43ed-ae7b-8cd22811f79f) to Jung (1a47f0a6-5217-4520-86e6-17164a254f2d) with sequence ID TECH-ANALYSIS-001:\\\\n\\\\n]Welcome, Director Jung. The user requires a website for their HOA. Key constraints are the user's non-technical background and the need for a simple admin interface. Your first task is to analyze potential technology stacks suitable for this project. Focus on solutions that prioritize ease of use for a non-technical administrator, such as a CMS (e.g., WordPress) or a hosted website builder (e.g., Squarespace). Your report should compare at least two distinct options, evaluating them on setup complexity, long-term maintenance, cost, security, and their ability to deliver the core features: 1) User login and role-based access to restricted content (meeting minutes, financial reports). 2) A blog/news section that is easy to update without code. 3) A contact form. Conclude with a clear recommendation for the best path forward. The user's original request was: 'I am very interested in building a website for my neighborhood HOA. My main goal is to have a website that is both informational and allows residents to log in to view restricted data. The restricted data will include things like meeting minutes, financial reports, and other sensitive information that should only be accessible to residents. Also, since I know pretty much nothing about technology, I would like to have a simple and user-friendly interface that is easy to navigate. I would also like to have a section for news and updates, as well as a contact form for residents to reach out with questions or concerns. Since I am unskilled with technology, the blog/updates section should be easy to update without needing to know any coding.'\\\"\\n}\\n###TECH-ANALYSIS-001###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###REPORT-TECH-ANALYSIS-001###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: Report\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reportString\\\": \\\"Technology Stack Analysis for HOA Website (per Order TECH-ANALYSIS-001)\\\\n\\\\n1. Overview of Core Requirements\\\\n   • Non-technical administrator must manage content and users.\\\\n   • Features: (a) resident log-in with role-based access to restricted documents; (b) blog/news section editable without code; (c) contact form.\\\\n\\\\n2. Candidate Solutions Compared\\\\n   A) WordPress.com (Business plan or higher)\\\\n   B) Squarespace (Business plan + Members Areas add-on)\\\\n\\\\n3. Evaluation Criteria\\\\n┌──────────────────────┬──────────────┬──────────────┐\\\\n│ Criterion            │ WordPress.com│ Squarespace  │\\\\n├──────────────────────┼──────────────┼──────────────┤\\\\n│ Initial Setup        │ Medium: choose host plan,   │ Low: guided wizard; theme\\\\n│                      │ pick theme, enable plugins. │ & basic pages live <1 hr. │\\\\n│ Long-Term Maintenance│ Core/platform auto-updated  │ Fully handled by platform.\\\\n│                      │ but plugin updates needed.  │ No updates required.      │\\\\n│ Cost (2024 USD)      │ Business $25/mo + domain    │ Business $23/mo + domain  │\\\\n│                      │ (≈$20/yr) + potential paid  │ + Members Areas tier:     │\\\\n│                      │ plugins ($0-15/mo).         │   • Starter $10/mo (100    │\\\\n│                      │                              │     members)\\\\n│                      │                              │   • Core $20/mo (1 k)\\\\n│ Security             │ Solid core security; risk   │ Closed system—attack      │\\\\n│                      │ scales with 3rd-party       │ surface smaller; vendor   │\\\\n│                      │ plugins (mitigated by       │ monitors patches.         │\\\\n│                      │ limiting plugin count).     │                          │\\\\n│ Restricted Content   │ Use MemberPress, WP-Members │ Members Areas fulfills;   │\\\\n│                      │ or similar plugin—fine-     │ easy page-level toggles.  │\\\\n│                      │ grained roles, unlimited    │                          │\\\\n│                      │ users.                      │                          │\\\\n│ Blog / News          │ Block editor (Gutenberg)    │ Integrated blog; drag-and │\\\\n│                      │ intuitive; scheduling, RSS. │ drop editor.              │\\\\n│ Contact Form         │ Built-in (Jetpack) or       │ Built-in Form Block with  │\\\\n│                      │ WPForms plugin.             │ email routing / storage.  │\\\\n│ Extendability        │ 60 k+ plugins; custom code  │ Limited—custom JS/CSS only│\\\\n│                      │ possible later.             │ within safe sandbox.      │\\\\n└──────────────────────┴──────────────┴──────────────┘\\\\n\\\\n4. Pros & Cons Summary\\\\nWordPress.com\\\\n  + Extremely flexible ecosystem; can evolve (forums, e-commerce, etc.).\\\\n  + Mature membership plugins with robust role controls.\\\\n  – Administrator must monitor occasional plugin updates/backups.\\\\n  – UI slightly busier; learning curve ~1-2 hrs.\\\\n\\\\nSquarespace\\\\n  + Turnkey; no maintenance burden for admin.\\\\n  + All functionality (including membership, blogging, forms) lives under one UI.\\\\n  – Members Areas add-on raises monthly cost; tiered by member count.\\\\n  – Less future extensibility; any advanced feature may require migrating later.\\\\n\\\\n5. Recommendation\\\\nGiven the user’s explicitly low technical comfort and the modest, well-defined feature set, Squarespace (Business plan + appropriate Members Areas tier) is the optimal path. It minimizes ongoing maintenance, offers an intuitive drag-and-drop editor for posts and pages, and secures restricted content without third-party plugins. Estimated recurring cost ≈ $33–$43/mo (incl. domain) for up to 1 000 residents—a predictable expense versus potential plugin or security overhead on WordPress.\\\\n\\\\nIf future needs expand toward complex integrations (e.g., custom workflows, large document repository), WordPress.com Business with MemberPress is the viable alternative, trading higher admin complexity for unlimited extensibility.\\\\n\\\\nActionable Next Step: Proceed to Planning Phase to outline a deterministic build/launch plan on Squarespace, covering account creation, template selection, Members Areas configuration, content migration structure, and hand-off documentation.\\\\n\\\",\\n  \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\": \\\"TECH-ANALYSIS-001\\\"\\n}\\n###REPORT-TECH-ANALYSIS-001###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###TECH-STANDBY-001###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Delta (7a7da8e1-4374-43ed-ae7b-8cd22811f79f)\\nTo: 1a47f0a6-5217-4520-86e6-17164a254f2d\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"[Orders from Delta (7a7da8e1-4374-43ed-ae7b-8cd22811f79f) to Jung (1a47f0a6-5217-4520-86e6-17164a254f2d) with sequence ID TECH-STANDBY-001:\\\\n\\\\n]Director Jung, we have now entered the Execution Phase. The approved plan involves the user setting up a Squarespace site. Stand by for potential orders related to troubleshooting technical issues that the user may encounter during this process.\\\"\\n}\\n###TECH-STANDBY-001###\"}]}],\"config\":{},\"tools\":[],\"output\":{}}}","genkit:state":"success"},"displayName":"generate","links":[],"instrumentationLibrary":{"name":"genkit-tracer","version":"v1"},"spanKind":"INTERNAL","sameProcessAsParentSpan":{"value":true},"status":{"code":0},"timeEvents":{"timeEvent":[]}}},"displayName":"generate","startTime":1752787518128,"endTime":1752787528000.4756}